WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.032
[SQUEAKING]
[RUSTLING] [CLICKING]

00:00:06.032 --> 00:00:07.490
VIVIAN SONG: In
this video, we will

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:09.680
be going over a Goodie
Bag number two which is

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:11.870
about electronic transitions.

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:14.180
What you'll need are four LEDs--

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:16.480
white, blue, green, and red--

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.130
and one spectrometer.

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:21.920
The objectives in this video are
to understand photon absorption

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.990
and emission, apply
the Bohr Model,

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:26.970
and use the spectrometer
to see sample spectra.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:29.450
The conceptual questions
you should keep in mind

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:32.990
are, how do electrons transition
to different energy levels?

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:37.160
And why is spectroscopy a method
of material characterization?

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:38.780
As a reminder,
the Bohr Model can

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:41.750
be used to model these
electronic transitions,

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:45.030
assuming that there's only
one electron in the atom.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:52.690
So right now, I'm going to
draw my energy axis and three

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:57.640
different energy levels that
my electron can hop into.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:02.290
So this is one,
and two, and three.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:06.310
So let's say that our electron
starts in the ground state.

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:09.790
And it absorbs
some energy and can

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:12.580
hop to a higher energy level.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:15.610
But in doing so, at this
higher energy level,

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:17.580
it is in a more unstable state.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:24.330
Eventually, it'll want to return
to that more stable state.

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:30.150
And when it does so, it emits
energy in the form of a photon.

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:36.950
So emit photon
and absorb energy.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:45.980
These different
electronic transitions

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:49.290
can be tracked
with this equation.

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:57.590
So the change in energy is equal
to minus 13.6z squared times 1

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:04.330
over nf squared minus
1 over ni squared.

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:08.060
Where z is the
atomic number, and f

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:09.800
is the final state
of the electron.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.350
And i is the initial
state of the electron.

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:16.840
And this change in energy
is in electron force.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:19.110
So remember that these
electronic transitions

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:22.710
are quantized since these energy
levels are not continuous,

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:24.540
but they're integers.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:27.240
This means that the photon
that the electron emits

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:33.370
is going to have a certain
set of different wavelengths.

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:37.125
And we can see that with
this equation energy equals

00:02:37.125 --> 00:02:43.810
hc over lambda, where energy is
equal to this change in energy.

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:45.870
h is Planck's constant.

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:47.180
c is the speed of light.

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:50.940
And lambda is the wavelength
of our emitted photon.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:53.520
After looking at the
red and white LEDs

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:56.230
through the spectrometer, you
should see something like this.

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:59.730
Notice how for the red LED,
mostly the red and orange bands

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:00.410
are visible.

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:03.000
Whereas for the white
LED, almost all the colors

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:04.170
are visible.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:05.760
Another thing that
you could explore

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:09.240
is using your spectrometer
to look at the ceiling lights

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:12.330
and seeing how that spectra
would differ from the spectra

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:15.090
that you observed
from your LEDs.

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:17.460
In summary, electrons
absorb and emit

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:21.030
photons of different wavelengths
because electronic transitions

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:21.980
are quantized.

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:24.360
By capturing this
information, the spectrometer

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:29.210
becomes a very useful tool for
characterizing our materials.

